  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement applies. `src/evidence.rs` `fn[validate_committed_credentials]` takes a parameter named `errors` and collects validation errors in it, so it is a site under `error_accumulators`.\n\nI judged both early-exit matches and found no gap:\n- **`return` in the `let ... else`:** It fires only when `load_discovery_sources` fails, before any check has run. This function has just one check, committed credentials in the Git index. The doc comment says an unreadable Git index is reported by discovery, so the failure is not lost and no later check is skipped. I did not open the discovery code to confirm this. The function also reports nothing outside Git, because nothing is committed there.\n- **`continue` in the `for` loop:** It skips a `habeas.toml` that cannot be read, cannot be parsed, or has no credential fields. It is the last step of that iteration's only check, so no later check in the same iteration is skipped. Other files are still examined, so one failure does not stop the run. An unparseable `habeas.toml` is its own failed check, and `credential_fields` returning `None` here does not stop the other checks.\n\nEach offending file gets exactly one pushed message, so no failure is reported twice. The message starts with the affected file path, names the failed check (committed explicit credentials), lists the offending setting names without their values, and says what to do instead. That matches `requirements.ci.validation_error`.",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "file": "src/evidence.rs",
      "item": "fn[validate_committed_credentials]",
      "hash": "9b102601eca4af75d5b34932a85447d2ff5dcbc631d0219c5c3c53a4953e99da"
